1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,575
A tug of war.

2
00:00:01,575 --> 00:00:02,995
A heavy school bag.

3
00:00:02,995 --> 00:00:04,771
A cut finger that bleeds.

4
00:00:05,171 --> 00:00:08,556
Each one uses tissues
that hold, pull or flow.

5
00:00:09,006 --> 00:00:12,076
Aarav lifted his heavy
bag and felt his arm work.

6
00:00:12,076 --> 00:00:14,765
He asked: what joins a muscle to a bone?

7
00:00:14,765 --> 00:00:17,594
And how can blood, a liquid, be a tissue?

8
00:00:17,944 --> 00:00:19,984
Two families of tissue answer that.

9
00:00:19,984 --> 00:00:22,499
Connective tissue, which
links and supports.

10
00:00:22,499 --> 00:00:25,109
And muscle tissue, which
contracts and pulls.

11
00:00:25,609 --> 00:00:29,879
By the end, you will know loose, dense
and specialised connective tissue,

12
00:00:29,879 --> 00:00:33,172
cartilage, bone and blood,
and three kinds of muscle.

13
00:00:33,822 --> 00:00:35,836
First, what makes a tissue connective?

14
00:00:36,186 --> 00:00:40,379
Connective tissue is the most common
and widely spread tissue in the body.

15
00:00:40,379 --> 00:00:43,109
It links and supports
other tissues and organs.

16
00:00:43,559 --> 00:00:48,165
Its cells, except in blood, make
protein fibres, collagen and elastin,

17
00:00:48,165 --> 00:00:50,522
and a jelly-like matrix around them.

18
00:00:50,522 --> 00:00:52,151
Collagen gives strength.

19
00:00:52,151 --> 00:00:53,839
Elastin lets it stretch.

20
00:00:54,489 --> 00:00:56,066
Let's zoom into Aarav's arm.

21
00:00:56,416 --> 00:01:00,286
From outside, the upper arm,
with a big muscle over the bone.

22
00:01:00,786 --> 00:01:02,568
Look inside near the elbow.

23
00:01:02,568 --> 00:01:06,977
The muscle ends in a tough white
cord, the tendon, that joins it to the

24
00:01:06,977 --> 00:01:07,877
bone.

25
00:01:08,002 --> 00:01:09,712
Zoom into the tendon.

26
00:01:09,712 --> 00:01:14,677
Long collagen fibres lie in neat
parallel rows, with fibroblast cells in

27
00:01:14,677 --> 00:01:15,577
between.

28
00:01:15,965 --> 00:01:18,496
This is dense regular connective tissue.

29
00:01:18,496 --> 00:01:22,840
Fibres packed in one direction make
it very strong when it is pulled.

30
00:01:23,490 --> 00:01:26,774
Now let's sort connective
tissue, step by step.

31
00:01:27,124 --> 00:01:28,024
Step one.

32
00:01:27,922 --> 00:01:32,319
Loose connective tissue has cells
and fibres loosely spread in a jelly.

33
00:01:32,319 --> 00:01:36,764
Areolar tissue, under the skin, is
a support framework for the skin.

34
00:01:37,514 --> 00:01:38,414
Step two.

35
00:01:38,312 --> 00:01:40,259
Adipose tissue is also loose.

36
00:01:40,259 --> 00:01:43,920
It lies mainly under the
skin, and its cells store fat.

37
00:01:44,670 --> 00:01:45,570
Step three.

38
00:01:45,492 --> 00:01:49,059
Dense connective tissue has fibres
and fibroblasts packed tight.

39
00:01:49,059 --> 00:01:50,802
Tendons join muscle to bone.

40
00:01:50,802 --> 00:01:52,545
Ligaments join bone to bone.

41
00:01:53,295 --> 00:01:54,195
Step four.

42
00:01:54,051 --> 00:01:55,790
Cartilage is solid but bendy.

43
00:01:55,790 --> 00:01:58,729
Its cells, the chondrocytes,
sit in small spaces.

44
00:01:58,729 --> 00:02:02,963
It is at the tip of the nose, in the
outer ear, and between the bones of

45
00:02:02,963 --> 00:02:03,863
the backbone.

46
00:02:04,600 --> 00:02:05,500
Step five.

47
00:02:05,403 --> 00:02:07,452
Bone is hard and does not bend.

48
00:02:07,452 --> 00:02:10,442
Its matrix is rich in
calcium salts and collagen.

49
00:02:10,442 --> 00:02:13,939
Its cells, the osteocytes,
sit in spaces called lacunae.

50
00:02:14,689 --> 00:02:15,760
Remember this.

51
00:02:15,760 --> 00:02:16,930
Cartilage bends.

52
00:02:16,930 --> 00:02:18,061
Bone does not.

53
00:02:18,661 --> 00:02:21,647
One connective tissue is a liquid: blood.

54
00:02:21,997 --> 00:02:24,427
Blood is a fluid connective tissue.

55
00:02:24,427 --> 00:02:26,754
Its liquid part is called plasma.

56
00:02:27,204 --> 00:02:31,170
In the plasma float red blood cells,
white blood cells and platelets.

57
00:02:31,620 --> 00:02:35,405
Blood is the main fluid that
carries substances around the body.

58
00:02:35,405 --> 00:02:39,531
And new blood cells are made in
the bone marrow, inside some bones.

59
00:02:40,181 --> 00:02:42,563
Now the second family: muscle tissue.

60
00:02:42,913 --> 00:02:46,545
A muscle is made of long,
thin fibres, side by side.

61
00:02:46,545 --> 00:02:50,559
Each fibre is packed with even
thinner threads, the myofibrils.

62
00:02:51,009 --> 00:02:53,665
When a signal arrives,
the fibres contract.

63
00:02:53,665 --> 00:02:56,139
They get shorter and thicker, and pull.

64
00:02:56,589 --> 00:02:58,983
Then they relax and get longer again.

65
00:02:58,983 --> 00:03:01,263
Contract, relax, again and again.

66
00:03:01,263 --> 00:03:03,762
That is how the biceps bends your arm.

67
00:03:04,412 --> 00:03:06,533
There are three kinds of muscle.

68
00:03:06,533 --> 00:03:09,978
Skeletal muscle is attached
to bones, like the biceps.

69
00:03:09,978 --> 00:03:14,210
Its fibres are striated, which
means striped, and we control them.

70
00:03:14,560 --> 00:03:19,286
Smooth muscle cells are spindle-shaped,
thin at both ends, with no stripes.

71
00:03:19,286 --> 00:03:23,681
They are in the walls of the stomach,
the intestine and blood vessels,

72
00:03:23,681 --> 00:03:25,622
and work without our control.

73
00:03:25,622 --> 00:03:27,310
We call this involuntary.

74
00:03:27,760 --> 00:03:30,228
Cardiac muscle is found only in the heart.

75
00:03:30,228 --> 00:03:34,074
Its cells are joined by intercalated
discs, so they contract together,

76
00:03:34,074 --> 00:03:34,974
as one unit.

77
00:03:35,572 --> 00:03:36,472
Your turn.

78
00:03:36,427 --> 00:03:40,476
Your stomach wall churns food,
and you never decide to do it.

79
00:03:40,476 --> 00:03:41,990
Which muscle is that?

80
00:03:41,990 --> 00:03:43,248
Pause and decide.

81
00:03:46,448 --> 00:03:47,425
Smooth muscle!

82
00:03:47,425 --> 00:03:50,758
It lines the stomach wall and
works without our control.

83
00:03:51,408 --> 00:03:52,937
Now, a worked example.

84
00:03:52,937 --> 00:03:54,095
Three body parts.

85
00:03:54,095 --> 00:03:55,959
Let's name the tissue in each.

86
00:03:56,309 --> 00:03:58,258
First, the tip of your nose.

87
00:03:58,258 --> 00:04:00,873
Press it, and it bends and springs back.

88
00:04:00,873 --> 00:04:02,009
Solid but bendy.

89
00:04:02,009 --> 00:04:02,909
Cartilage.

90
00:04:03,207 --> 00:04:06,596
Second, the knee, where a cord
joins one bone to another.

91
00:04:06,596 --> 00:04:07,990
Dense connective tissue.

92
00:04:07,990 --> 00:04:08,890
A ligament.

93
00:04:09,227 --> 00:04:13,054
Third, the soft layer under the
skin, where cells store fat.

94
00:04:13,504 --> 00:04:16,497
That is adipose tissue, a
loose connective tissue.

95
00:04:17,147 --> 00:04:18,609
Now, the exam trap.

96
00:04:18,959 --> 00:04:21,279
Many students mix up
tendons and ligaments.

97
00:04:21,679 --> 00:04:23,877
A tendon joins muscle to bone.

98
00:04:23,877 --> 00:04:26,074
A ligament joins bone to bone.

99
00:04:26,074 --> 00:04:28,844
Both are dense regular connective tissue.

100
00:04:29,494 --> 00:04:30,841
Where is this asked?

101
00:04:30,841 --> 00:04:34,918
As statement or match questions on
tendons, ligaments, cartilage and the

102
00:04:34,918 --> 00:04:36,356
three kinds of muscle.

103
00:04:36,956 --> 00:04:38,117
Exam level now.

104
00:04:38,117 --> 00:04:39,829
Read the four statements.

105
00:04:39,829 --> 00:04:41,443
Which ones are correct?

106
00:04:41,443 --> 00:04:42,654
Pause and solve.

107
00:04:48,054 --> 00:04:49,406
The answer is B.

108
00:04:49,406 --> 00:04:51,177
Statements two and four.

109
00:04:51,677 --> 00:04:52,668
One is wrong.

110
00:04:52,668 --> 00:04:54,458
Tendons join muscle to bone.

111
00:04:54,458 --> 00:04:55,540
Three is wrong.

112
00:04:55,540 --> 00:04:57,320
Smooth muscle is involuntary.

113
00:04:57,820 --> 00:04:59,299
Two and four are right.

114
00:04:59,299 --> 00:05:03,221
Adipose tissue stores fat, and
intercalated discs let heart cells contract

115
00:05:03,221 --> 00:05:04,121
as a unit.

116
00:05:04,630 --> 00:05:06,171
Here is your recap.

117
00:05:06,171 --> 00:05:07,755
Screenshot this card.

118
00:05:10,955 --> 00:05:13,141
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

119
00:05:13,541 --> 00:05:14,441
One.

120
00:05:13,959 --> 00:05:15,477
What does a ligament join?

121
00:05:16,377 --> 00:05:17,277
Two.

122
00:05:16,814 --> 00:05:19,154
Name the cells of cartilage and of bone.

123
00:05:20,054 --> 00:05:20,954
Three.

124
00:05:20,523 --> 00:05:22,622
Which muscle is found only in the heart?

125
00:05:23,722 --> 00:05:28,155
Next time: a frog never drinks
water, yet it never dries out.

126
00:05:28,155 --> 00:05:29,055
How?

127
00:05:28,691 --> 00:05:31,873
We find out in frog,
morphology and anatomy.
